cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "chess-lab: falsifiable analyses of public chess games against engine ground truth"
message: "If you use these findings or methods, please cite this repository. If you cite a null result, cite its resolution bound with it."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Morrissey
    given-names: Andrew
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0004-8758-2687"
repository-code: "https://github.com/ASM-21/chess-lab"
url: "https://github.com/ASM-21/chess-lab"
version: 1.2.0
date-released: "2026-10-01"
license: Apache-2.0
keywords:
  - chess
  - stockfish
  - lichess
  - reproducible-research
  - falsification
  - specification-curve
abstract: >-
  Nine claims about chess tested on 190,967 public games (a 2017 lichess.org
  scrape and a 1998-2026 sample of The Week In Chess), using Stockfish 16 at a
  fixed node budget as the measuring instrument, and positioned against the
  published literature, with an independent replication on 165,852 chess.com
  Titled Tuesday games. The most defensibly new result is that lost positions
  are saved far more often online than over the board because over-the-board
  players concede: linking the same 730 titled players by FIDE ID across two
  unrelated sources, 16.6% of their online losses end in checkmate against 4.3%
  of their classical over-the-board losses, and the gap holds when both are
  blitz. About 30% of their online losses are time forfeits. Other results
  replicate known findings on blunder rates, White's advantage and rating drift,
  or are consistent with recent work on draw rates and opening evaluation. The
  repository includes a falsification track with eight probes, specification
  curves with joint inference, and a stated resolution for every null. Seven
  earlier claims were withdrawn, three of them after a literature search showed
  the tournament corpus had become majority online blitz from 2020.
